WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:08.910
Hello, welcome!
In this AI Coffee Break, we are going to see

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:15.639
how a language model, usually applied on natural
language, can solve biology problems!

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:19.590
Is this not at least a little bit crazy?

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:25.619
So far in the Transformer series, we have
seen how we can use Transformer-based language

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:34.250
models like BERT or GPT-2, GPT-3 on text.
Or on images, or even on both!

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:40.460
But what if Ms. Coffee Bean tells you, we
can use Transformers for analysing protein

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.620
structure!

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:47.240
So in this video, we will explain what the
biological problem is and why we can solve

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:51.789
it with a transformer model originally meant
for natural language!

00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:58.010
This method we are talking about, was published
quite recently. Oh, come on, who are we kidding,

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.450
it was published one month ago!

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.990
One month ago in Machine Learning, is way
old!

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:11.460
Anyway, let’s get back to the subject: What
does this paper do? It takes BERT, a Transformer

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.030
model, like we presented it in a previous
video, linked below.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:22.640
We remember, BERT is trained on masked word
prediction and one of the most important internal

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.220
mechanisms is self-attention.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:31.659
Self-attention computes importance scores
between tokens (words or subwords) in the

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:34.260
same sequence.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.750
Attention computes importance scores between
tokens from different sequences.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:45.240
And we do not compute one attention per sequence,
but multiple attention scores, called attention

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.070
heads, here color-coded.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:53.560
By learning to predict words in natural language,
the model learns more complex relationships,

00:01:53.560 --> 00:02:00.880
present in language, like for example parts
of speech co-reference, sentiment and so on.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.420
Because Bert is notorious for learning implicit
patterns in the data, for discovering structure

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:12.790
that is not even annotated, a whole sub-field
emerged, called BERTology: What does the BERT

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.700
model learn about natural language?

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:21.580
A method of finding out if BERT learned something
about another task B, while being trained

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:27.720
on task A, is called probing. We discussed
it in a previous video, linked in the description

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:28.720
below.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:35.579
Until now, we have explained the BERTology
part of the title. Where is the biology?

00:02:35.579 --> 00:02:38.299
Ah, here it is!

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:43.020
In the central dogma of biology, there are
three important pieces that play a role in

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:50.640
how cells live and reproduce: the DNA, the
RNA and the proteins. For the sake of this

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:59.180
video, we can simplify this by saying DNA
is the blueprint of the cell, whereas proteins

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:03.690
are the machinery built from this blueprint.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:11.220
The DNA contains a sequence of bases often
encoded as the letters ATG and C. This sequence

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:18.190
is then transcribed into RNA and then translated
further into the language of amino acids.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:23.500
Amino acids are just molecules , but when
connected into a long string, they form proteins.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:31.820
Similarly, to how we encode bases as the letters
A T G and C, we can also encode the 20 amino-acids

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:37.810
with letters from the alphabet. This amino-acid
sequence is important for a well-functioning

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:43.870
protein. But some amino acids have very similar
chemical properties and could be exchanged

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:47.260
without breaking the viability of the protein.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:54.770
Until now, we regarded proteins as a 2D sequence
of amino-acids. Only that proteins exist in

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:57.060
3D space, how else?

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:02.400
So important for the function of the protein,
is not only the amino-acid sequence, but also

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.419
the three-dimensional shape that this long
line of molecules takes. Biologists call this

00:04:08.419 --> 00:04:12.290
the secondary and tertiary structure.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:19.359
The tertiary structure is the overall shape
of the protein, the secondary structure describes

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:26.300
recurring sub-shapes such as turns and folds,
helices, and sheets.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:32.450
Because proteins are folded into themselves
in complicated 3D shapes, some amino acids

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:38.600
are very close to each other in 3D space,
even though they are far away from each other

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.030
in the 2D sequence.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:45.310
And proteins don’t all work by themselves,
but sometimes they interact with

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:50.580
each other and bind at well defined binding
sites.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:57.870
Equipped with this biological knowledge, let’s
look at how BERT could help in protein biology.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:04.090
In natural language, we have text, which is
a sequence of words. These words could form

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:10.560
simple or more complicated meanings and patterns,
for example a simple pattern would be to identify

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.000
that different words have a different part
of speech.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:20.070
One is a verb, the other one a noun and these
patterns are not explicitly marked or annotated,

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:23.380
but they contribute heavily to conveying meaning.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:28.210
More complex patterns would be co-reference
or sentiment, and so on.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:33.750
On the other side, we have protein sequences,
where the “words” are the amino acids.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:39.440
Similarly, they also have intrinsic structural
properties like the secondary structure, the

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.470
tertiary structure or the binding sites.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:49.050
So let us see, if BERT that works on language
and identifies the inner structure of language

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:54.860
with the attention mechanism so well, can
also work on these sequences of amino acids

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:59.600
and tell us more about the inner biological
workings of the cell!

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:06.340
The idea then would be to train BERT like
we train usually on language, by masked language

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:13.080
modelling: We take the amino acid sequence,
mask a portion of them and let BERT train

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.920
while predicting what the masked amino-acid
might be!

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:23.740
And this task would be for BERT quite simple:
While English has a vocabulary of around 10,000

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:29.590
words, we have only about 20 amino-acids to
consider, should be easy, right?

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:34.480
Well, it's not that easy, because we want
to also learn something about interesting

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:40.620
biology here. Reconstructing missing amino
acids in the sequence is not our main goal.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.990
So for detecting interesting properties, like
secondary and tertiary structures or binding

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:51.210
sites, the authors are analyzing the attention
patterns.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:55.830
They do this by measuring the correlation
of the attention of each head to different

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:59.090
properties of the amino acid.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:04.450
Like in natural language, some attention heads
in BERT focus on only one specific word, for

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:10.970
example “the” or “I” in a sentence.
Here, for proteins, we have a head focusing

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:15.400
on the occurrence of only one amino-acid,
for example proline.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:20.500
More interesting properties can be interpreted
from this result. Attention heads that focus

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:26.900
on one amino-acid, also have a high attention
score for amino-acids with which they can

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:29.090
be substituted with.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:34.810
So amino acids with similar chemical properties
share high attentions! This is similar to

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:38.900
what we observe with synonyms in natural language!

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:44.639
We can also find attention heads that align
with the contact maps, meaning that in the

00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:48.940
three dimensional structure, these amino acids
are close to each other.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:55.570
The authors saw that this head’s attention
is a very well calibrated estimator for the

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:57.310
probability of contact.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:02.550
For binding sites this attention gets more
complicated, since binding sites are more

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:07.730
difficult to describe biologically, because
there are many different types of binding

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:14.630
sites. This is why, there are more heads working
together on describing this phenomenon.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:21.010
This paper is a nice proof of concept that
many sequence-based data types can be processed

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:26.419
by the Transformer architecture. Without any
explicit annotation, the model figures out

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:29.330
interesting phenomena by itself!

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:34.079
If you liked this video, please tell everybody
about Ms. Coffee Bean, and do not forget to

00:08:34.079 --> 00:08:38.120
hit the subscribe and like buttons! Bye!

